WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.103
Humans have been making
silk for centuries.

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:07.430
It's a coveted textile for
gowns, sheets, and robes.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.846
But why is it so expensive?

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.359
Silk is the product

00:00:14.359 --> 00:00:17.411
of the domesticated silkworm, Bombyx mori.

00:00:17.411 --> 00:00:20.588
The caterpillars eat constantly.

00:00:20.588 --> 00:00:23.547
They're fed mulberry
leaves five times a day.

00:00:23.547 --> 00:00:26.551
After 45 days of munching and growing,

00:00:26.551 --> 00:00:28.780
the worms are ready to mature.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.500
They have a special
spinneret that secretes

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.280
one long continuous filament.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.820
That's the silk.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.069
The silkworms spin this
filament into a cocoon.

00:00:39.069 --> 00:00:42.810
Then the caterpillar
begins its transformation.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.060
Except, this is when
silk producers butt in.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.050
The cocoons are steamed.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.310
This cleans them and
kills the pupa inside.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.140
If the pupas were kept alive,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.610
they would degrade and break the silk.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.190
Then the cocoons are unraveled

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.662
to reveal around half a
mile of usable filament.

00:01:00.662 --> 00:01:03.360
Filaments are joined to
create a thread thick enough

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.854
to weave into fabric.

00:01:05.854 --> 00:01:10.006
One yard of silk fabric
costs approximately $100.

00:01:10.006 --> 00:01:13.369
What makes it worth the cost?

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:16.083
We spoke to a few experts
on the subject to find out.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.890
Shawne Jacobs is the creative director

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:23.557
and president of wedding dress
design house, Anne Barge.

00:01:29.242 --> 00:01:31.670
Consumers accustomed
to synthetic materials

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.670
like polyester expect perfection.

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:58.370
Professor Daniel Nicodemo
credits silk's price tag

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.051
to its thermal conductivity
and sturdiness.

00:02:01.051 --> 00:02:04.800
It's also easily dyed and
doesn't cause skin irritation.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.460
So what do you think?

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.391
Is silk worth the cost?

